[Event "Pawn endgames: the opposition"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Role "teaching"]
[IntendedResult "win"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/J7JDkozw/CJM4mA13"]
[FEN "8/4k3/8/4K3/4P3/8/8/8 b - - 0 1"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/8/4k3/8/4K3/4P3/8/8/8_b_-_-_0_1"]

{Welcome to pawn endings. Our first word is opposition: two kings facing each other with one square between them, where whoever has to move must give way.}
1... Kd7 {His king had to give way — the kings stood opposed, and any king move breaks that — so he went to d7, the queenside square. Cross to f6 and get round him. [%cal Ye5f6]} 2. Kf6 {Your king has slipped round to the far side of his. He went queenside to d7, so the kingside road to e8 is yours, and he can never get back in front of the pawn. [%csl Ge8]} (2. Kd5 {Taking the opposition again on d5 also wins, since your king still ends up in front of the pawn — but crossing to f6 gets there quicker.} 2... Ke7 {He steps back to e7 and faces you again. You still have to outflank him from here, so the win takes longer than it needed to.}) (2. Kf5 {Kf5 also wins, but it is a sideways step. Your king wants to go forwards, straight at the pawn's road.} 2... Ke7 {He returns to e7, right back in front of the pawn, and you have spent a move without gaining any ground.}) (2. Kd4 {Stepping back to d4, behind your own pawn, throws the win away. Never retreat when your king is already ahead of it.} 2... Kd6 {He steps to d6 and takes the opposition himself. Now he can never be pushed off the pawn's road, and the win is gone.}) (2. Kf4 {The same mistake on the other wing: Kf4 drops your king back behind the pawn again, and the win goes with it.} 2... Ke6 {He plants his king on e6, right in front of the pawn, and holds it there forever. Only a draw now.}) 2... Kd8 {He falls straight back to d8, his best try. Now take the square right beside him, on f7. [%cal Yf6f7]} 3. Kf7 {Your king reaches f7, right beside e8 where the pawn will queen, and he can never come near that square again. Now watch the pawn walk home. [%csl Ge8][%cal Ge4e8][%tail]} (3. e5 {Pushing to e5 first still wins, but it is impatient. Let your king lead the way, and the pawn can follow behind it.} 3... Ke8 {He sits down in front of it on e8, and you still have to find the same king walk you already know works.}) (3. Ke6 {Ke6 also wins, since your king still reaches the pawn's road — but f7 gets there in one move instead of two.} 3... Kc7 {He dashes off to the other wing instead, and now you have more walking to do before the pawn is safe.}) (3. Kf5 {Dropping back to f5 undoes the ground you had already won: your king steps away from e8 instead of guarding it.} 3... Ke7 {He marches straight back to e7, right in front of the pawn, and you have to climb the same ladder again.}) (3. Ke5 {Ke5 plants your own king in front of your own pawn. It blocks the pawn's road forward, and the win disappears.} 3... Ke7 {He takes the opposition on e7 and holds it. Your king and pawn can never get past him now. Only a draw.}) 3... Kc7 4. e5 4... Kd7 5. e6+ 5... Kc7 6. e7 6... Kb7 7. e8=Q {A new queen stands on e8, and his king never came within three squares of it. That is opposition and the outflank, cashed in full. [%csl Ge8]} 1-0


[Event "Pawn endgames: do not push too soon"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Role "exercise"]
[IntendedResult "win"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/J7JDkozw/rquahaDY"]
[FEN "5k2/8/5K2/6P1/8/8/8/8 b - - 0 1"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/5k2/8/5K2/6P1/8/8/8/8_b_-_-_0_1"]

{Same idea as last time: opposition, kings facing off with one square between them, and it is his move. Only one square for your king wins this one.}
1... Kg8 {He gives way into the corner, on g8. Whichever square your king takes now decides whether the pawn ever queens.} 2. Kg6 {Opposition again: your king takes the square right in front of his, and he has to step aside for it. [%csl Gg6,Gg8]} (2. g6 {Running the pawn to g6 straight away throws the win away. Bring your king in first; a lone pawn against his king can never get through.} 2... Kf8 3. g7+ Kg8 {His king simply sits on g8, right in front of the pawn, and he can never be shifted from there. Drawn. [%csl Rg8]}) (2. Ke6 {Crossing to e6 worked with a centre pawn, but a knight pawn is different: stepping off its own column lets his king back in front of it.} 2... Kg7 {He slips onto g7, right in front of the pawn again, and from there it can never get past him. Drawn.}) (2. Ke7 {Ke7 repeats the wrong idea: it leaves the g column altogether, and his king simply comes back to block the pawn.} 2... Kg7 {His king reaches g7 and blocks the pawn cold. The win you had is gone.}) (2. Ke5 {Ke5 steps backwards, away from the pawn, and leaves it to fend for itself with no king there to help it through.} 2... Kf7 {He marches straight up to the pawn on f7 and holds it there for good. Drawn.}) (2. Kf5 {A step back to f5 gives up the opposition you had just won, and with it the whole win.} 2... Kf7 {He faces you again from f7, and your pawn will never get past him now.}) 2... Kf8 {He gives way to f8, off the g column. Which square lets your king walk all the way to the queening corner?} 3. Kh7 {Kh7 tucks your king right next to the queening corner on g8, and he can never get near it again. Now watch the g pawn walk home. [%csl Gg8][%cal Gg5g8][%tail]} (3. Kf6 {Kf6 still wins, but it only returns you to the square you started from — no ground gained on this move.} 3... Kg8 {He returns to the corner on g8, exactly where he began. Kf6 still wins the ending, but that move gained no ground at all.}) (3. Kh6 {Kh6 wins too, just more slowly: it costs an extra move before your king can reach the corner square you actually need.} 3... Kg8 {He heads back into the corner on g8, and you still have to find h7 from here to finish the job.}) (3. Kf5 {Stepping back to f5 lets go of the king walk you had built. The moment you retreat, the win goes with it.} 3... Kf7 {He plants his king on f7, facing yours, and the pawn can never get past him now. Drawn.}) (3. Kh5 {Kh5 retreats again, off the file entirely, and now he simply steps in front of the pawn without a fight.} 3... Kg7 {His king takes g7, right in front of the pawn, and holds it there. A draw.}) 3... Ke8 4. g6 4... Kf8 5. g7+ 5... Kf7 6. g8=Q+ {A new queen stands on g8, with check. Your king walked the pawn all the way home, and his king never got within reach of it.} 1-0


[Event "Pawn endgames: go the long way round"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Role "exercise"]
[IntendedResult "win"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/J7JDkozw/f9IbrZAY"]
[FEN "8/8/5k2/2P5/2K5/8/8/8 b - - 0 1"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/8/8/5k2/2P5/2K5/8/8/8_b_-_-_0_1"]

{His king is sprinting back to stop your pawn, and only one road gets your king there first. Take your time finding it.}
1... Ke7 {He races back toward the pawn, running to e7. Which square lets your king reach the pawn's road ahead of him?} 2. Kb5 {Round the side! Your king heads for b5 while the pawn waits, so the king leads the way instead of running alone. [%csl Gb5]} (2. c6 {Sending the pawn to c6 alone throws the win away: it runs straight into his king with no support at all.} 2... Kd6 {He steps in front on d6, and your king is too far off on the wrong side to ever dislodge him. Drawn.}) (2. Kd5 {Kd5 goes straight ahead and lets him simply take the opposition. Facing you on d7, he can never be pushed aside.} 2... Kd7 {Now you cannot get past him on d7: he sits right in front of the pawn, and it can never move again. Only a draw.}) (2. Kb4 {Kb4 has the right idea, going round the side, but it is one square short: his king gets home just in time.} 2... Kd7 {He gets back to d7 just in time, and the win you were chasing has slipped away.}) (2. Kd4 {Kd4 steps backwards, away from the race entirely, and the win goes with it.} 2... Ke6 {He walks straight up to the pawn on e6 and holds it there for good. Drawn.}) 2... Kd7 {He gets in front of the pawn on d7. Which square finishes the job from here?} 3. Kb6 {Kb6 is the key square: from here your pawn can never be stopped again. Now watch it walk home. [%csl Gb6][%cal Gc5c8][%tail]} (3. c6+ {Pushing to c6 with check feels active, but it costs you the win: his king simply blocks on c7 and the pawn is stuck for good.} 3... Kc7 {He blocks on c7 with his king, and your pawn on c5 never gets to move again. Drawn.}) (3. Ka6 {Ka6 swings too far round the side, leaving the pawn to fend for itself with no king behind it.} 3... Kc6 {He walks straight up to c6, and the pawn falls to his king with nobody there to defend it. Drawn.}) (3. Ka5 {Ka5 goes sideways instead of forwards, and the win goes with the wasted move.} 3... Kc6 {He reaches the pawn first, arriving on c6 before your king can help, and it simply falls. Drawn.}) (3. Kc4 {Kc4 steps backwards again, giving up the ground your king had gained, and he simply strolls up to meet the pawn on c6.} 3... Kc6 {He stands in front of the pawn once more, on c6, and you have nothing left to play for.}) 3... Kc8 4. Kc6 4... Kb8 5. Kd7 5... Kb7 6. c6+ 6... Kb8 7. c7+ 7... Kb7 8. c8=Q+ {A new queen stands on c8, with check. His king never got within two squares of stopping it.} 1-0


[Event "Pawn endgames: the moment to push"]
[Result "0-1"]
[Role "exercise"]
[IntendedResult "win"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/J7JDkozw/Mqbt5bP4"]
[FEN "8/8/8/8/2p1k3/8/5K2/8 w - - 0 1"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/8/8/8/8/2p1k3/8/5K2/8_w_-_-_0_1"]

{You are Black now, a pawn up, with his king rushing back to stop yours. One move decides the whole game.}
1. Ke2 {He plants his king on e2, right in front of your pawn. Whatever you play next decides the whole game.} 1... c3 {Push to c3! His king can never set foot on c2 again, so your pawn always has a road through. [%csl Gc2]} (1... Kd4 {Bringing the king first with Kd4 also wins, but it hands him a free move he did not need to be given.} 2. Kd2 {He drops his king onto d2, dead in front of the pawn, and it can never take a single step. Drawn.}) (1... Kf4 {Kf4 walks your king away from its own pawn, leaving it with nobody there to help it through.} 2. Kd2 {He blocks the pawn on d2, and it is stuck there for the rest of the game.}) (1... Kd5 {Kd5 is too slow here: the pawn is the one that has to lead this race, not your king.} 2. Kd2 {He plants himself right in front of the pawn on d2, and there is nothing left for you to play for.}) (1... Ke5 {Ke5 steps backwards, and the one moment you had to push the pawn through is gone.} 2. Kd2 {He takes c2 away from your pawn, and it can never pass him now. Drawn.}) 2. Ke1 {He falls back to the last row, onto e1. Which square do you want your king to take now?} 2... Ke3 {Opposition on e3: his king has to give way, and your pawn walks straight through on c2. Now watch it queen. [%csl Ge3,Ge1][%cal Gc3c1][%tail]} (2... Kd4 {Kd4 also wins, but it gives him one more move to fight with. Taking the opposition on e3 was the faster road.} 3. Ke2 {He fights on with Ke2, but the opposition you could have taken on e3 would have finished him quicker.}) 3. Kd1 3... Kd3 4. Kc1 4... c2 5. Kb2 5... Kd2 6. Ka3 6... c1=Q+ {A new queen stands on c1, with check. His king never made it back in time to stop it.} 0-1


[Event "Pawn endgames: the rule of the square"]
[Result "0-1"]
[Role "teaching"]
[IntendedResult "draw"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/J7JDkozw/nG3HbTy0"]
[FEN "7K/8/8/8/P3k3/8/8/8 w - - 0 1"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/7K/8/8/8/P3k3/8/8/8_w_-_-_0_1"]

{You have no pawn, so holding the draw means catching his. A new word: the square. His pawn draws a box as it runs, and your king must always stay inside it. [%cal Ga4a8,Ga8e8,Ge8e4,Ge4a4]}
1. a5 {The pawn steps to a5, and the box shrinks with it: now a5 to a8, across to d8, and down to d5. Step inside that box. [%cal Ga5a8,Ga8d8,Gd8d5,Gd5a5][%cal Ye4d5]} 1... Kd5 {Kd5 lands inside the square, and from here every push he makes keeps you inside it too. [%csl Gd5]} (1... Kd4 {Kd4 chases the pawn instead of cutting across its box. Count the squares again before you move.} 2. a6 Kc3 3. a7 {The box you needed was one row higher. His pawn queens on a8, and you are lost. [%csl Rc3][%cal Ra7a8,Ra8b8,Rb8b7,Rb7a7]}) (1... Ke5 {Ke5 goes sideways, and the box does not move with you: it still runs from a5, and this step leaves you outside it. [%csl Re5][%cal Ra5a8,Ra8d8,Rd8d5,Rd5a5]} 2. a6 Kd4 3. a7 {You are two squares short of the corner square this time, and there is no catching up now. He queens next move.}) (1... Kd3 {Kd3 steps backwards, and the pawn runs completely out of your king's reach.} 2. a6 Kc2 3. a7 {The pawn reaches the last row and queens, and your king is left outside the box, able only to watch.}) 2. a6 {He pushes again to a6, and the box shrinks with him: keep following it down the diagonal. [%cal Ga6a8,Ga8c8,Gc8c6,Gc6a6][%cal Yd5c6]} 2... Kc6 {Kc6 keeps you inside the square. Your king is walking the diagonal straight home to the corner.} (2... Kd6 {Kd6 is one step too slow, and it leaves you outside the box he has just drawn. [%csl Rd6][%cal Ra6a8,Ra8c8,Rc8c6,Rc6a6]} 3. a7 Kc5 4. a8=Q {The new queen arrives on a8 with nobody there to challenge her, and you are lost.}) (2... Kc5 {Kc5 has the right column but the wrong row: you are still one square outside the box.} 3. a7 Kb4 4. a8=Q {That queen arrives on a8 a single move too late for your king to ever catch her.}) (2... Kc4 {Kc4 drops down instead of crossing over, and the box leaves your king behind it. [%csl Rc4][%cal Ra6a8,Ra8c8,Rc8c6,Rc6a6]} 3. a7 Kb3 4. a8=Q {You are one move too slow to reach the corner, and the pawn queens on a8 unopposed.}) 3. a7 {One more push, to a7. Now one more step and your king meets the pawn right at the corner. [%cal Ga7a8,Ga8b8,Gb8b7,Gb7a7][%cal Yc6b7]} 3... Kb7 {Caught it! Your king on b7 covers a8, the only square left for his pawn, so now watch him push it anyway. [%tail][%csl Gb7][%cal Gb7a8]} (3... Kb6 {Kb6 is close, but b7 is the square that actually meets the pawn head on.} 4. a8=Q Kb5 {A whole queen stands against your bare king now, and a lone king can never hold that off. Lost.}) (3... Kc7 {Kc7 misses it by one square: you need to stand right in front of the pawn, on b7 itself.} 4. a8=Q Kb6 {The pawn makes it all the way home and queens, with your king still a step away from the corner.}) 4. a8=Q+ 4... Kxa8 {Your king takes the new queen on a8 the instant she appears. Nothing is left but two bare kings, and that is a draw. [%csl Ga8]} 0-1


[Event "Pawn endgames: run for the corner"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Role "review"]
[IntendedResult "draw"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/J7JDkozw/s69UVI17"]
[FEN "8/1K5p/8/8/8/8/8/k7 w - - 0 1"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/8/1K5p/8/8/8/8/8/k7_w_-_-_0_1"]

{You have no pawn again, so holding the draw means catching his. His h pawn has not moved, so it can jump two squares at once: count the square from h6, not h7. [%csl Gh6]}
1. Kc6 {Only the diagonal move works, and it has to be now: from h6 the square only reaches as far as c6. [%csl Gc6][%cal Gh6h1,Gh1c1,Gc1c6,Gc6h6]} (1. Kb6 {Kb6 goes sideways and costs a step you can never win back. That one tempo is the whole game.} 1... h5 2. Kc5 h4 {His pawn steps outside the square and now queens freely. You are lost. [%csl Rc5][%cal Rh4h1,Rh1e1,Re1e4,Re4h4]}) (1. Kc7 {Kc7 goes along the row instead of the diagonal, and that costs a whole move you cannot spare.} 1... h5 2. Kd6 h4 {Too slow: your king cannot reach the square in time, and that pawn can never be caught now.}) (1. Kb8 {Kb8 heads the wrong way entirely, and there is no time left to spare in a race this close.} 1... h5 2. Kc7 h4 {The pawn is long gone down the row by the time your king finally arrives there.}) 1... h5 {There it is, two squares in one jump. Good thing you counted the square from h6.} 2. Kd5 {Kd5 keeps you on the diagonal, still shrinking the head start his pawn had. [%cal Gh5h1,Gh1d1,Gd1d5,Gd5h5]} (2. Kd6 {Kd6 is one row too high, and it breaks the diagonal your king needed to stay on.} 2... h4 3. Kc5 h3 {The pawn reaches h1 and queens, with your king still two whole squares away. Lost.}) (2. Kc5 {Kc5 drops your king down instead of across, off the diagonal it was riding.} 2... h4 3. Kb4 h3 {Your king is nowhere near catching it now, and the pawn simply runs on unopposed.}) (2. Kd7 {Kd7 steps backwards, and every single step matters in a race this close.} 2... h4 3. Kc6 h3 {The pawn is gone down its own road, and the draw you were chasing goes with it.}) 2... h4 {He keeps running, to h4. One more step and your king is there to meet it.} 3. Ke4 {Your king reaches e4, inside the square at last, and that h pawn is caught for good. Now watch the chase finish. [%csl Ge4][%cal Gh4h1,Gh1e1,Ge1e4,Ge4h4][%tail]} (3. Kd4 {Kd4 is the wrong diagonal, and the pawn slips straight past your king's reach.} 3... h3 4. Kc3 h2 {It queens on the very next move, with your king still outside the square.}) (3. Ke5 {Ke5 is too high. Count the squares again before you commit your king.} 3... h3 4. Kd4 h2 {That queen arrives on h1 one single move too soon for your king to ever stop it.}) (3. Ke6 {Ke6 only takes your king further away, not closer to the pawn's road.} 3... h3 4. Kd5 h2 {The pawn reaches h1 and queens there, and you are lost.}) 3... h3 4. Kf3 4... h2 5. Kg2 5... h1=Q+ 6. Kxh1 {His pawn reaches h1 and turns into a queen, and your king eats her on the spot. Two bare kings are left, and neither can mate. Drawn. [%csl Gh1]} 1-0


[Event "Pawn endgames: two jobs at once"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Role "exercise"]
[IntendedResult "draw"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/J7JDkozw/ibEe7Um6"]
[FEN "7K/8/k1P5/7p/8/8/8/8 w - - 0 1"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/7K/8/k1P5/7p/8/8/8/8_w_-_-_0_1"]

{Both of you have a pawn racing to queen, and your job is to hold the draw, not chase a win. Your king looks miles away, but one square does both jobs. Take your time.}
1. Kg7 {Kg7 heads toward both jobs at once: chasing his h pawn and walking home to help your own on c6.} (1. c7 {Pushing to c7 straight away is too quick: the pawn runs straight into his king with no support behind it.} 1... Kb7 2. c8=Q+ Kxc8 {You gave the pawn away for nothing, and now his own runs home unopposed. Lost.}) (1. Kh7 {Kh7 goes straight at the pawn, and that single step is too slow to also help your own.} 1... Kb6 2. Kg6 h4 {He wins the race for the h pawn, and your own pawn is left with nobody to help it. Lost.}) (1. Kg8 {Kg8 heads down the wrong road: the diagonal toward g7 was the square that did both jobs at once.} 1... Kb6 2. Kf7 Kxc6 {His king eats your pawn on c6, and then his own runs home to queen. Lost.}) 1... h4 {He runs, pushing to h4. Stay on the diagonal and keep doing both jobs.} 2. Kf6 {Kf6 keeps both jobs going: his pawn is still ahead of you, and yours is getting closer with every step.} (2. c7 {The same rush as before, and the same result: the pawn runs into his king alone.} 2... Kb7 3. c8=Q+ Kxc8 {A pawn given away on f7 for nothing, and his own runner queens right behind it. Lost.}) (2. Kg6 {Kg6 steps sideways, and the diagonal that was doing both jobs is gone.} 2... Kb6 3. Kf5 h3 {From here you can catch his pawn or help your own, but never both at once.}) (2. Kf7 {Kf7 drops off the diagonal, so now your king can only do one job at a time.} 2... Kb6 3. Ke6 Kxc6 {Your own pawn on c6 falls with no king to guard it, and his h pawn runs all the way home. Lost.}) 2... h3 {Still running, to h3. Now your king turns for home to finish the second job.} 3. Ke6 {Your king stands beside its own pawn at last, though his runner is still out ahead of you. Two jobs, one king. [%csl Gc6,Ge6]} (3. Ke7 {Ke7 is just as good: your pawn still gets its escort either way you approach it.} 3... h2 4. c7 h1=Q {Both sides queen here, and the game is level: a drawn queen ending, not a loss.}) (3. c7 {Pushing to c7 here is once more too early, and once more his king is sitting right there waiting for it.} 3... Kb7 4. c8=Q+ Kxc8 {Lost. He takes the new queen the moment she is born, and now his own h pawn runs home with nobody watching it.}) (3. Kf5 {Kf5 chases the h pawn alone, and your own pawn on c6 is left with no help at all.} 3... Kb6 4. Ke4 h2 {From f5 your king can only chase his pawn; there is nobody left to shepherd your own home.}) 3... h2 {He drops his pawn to h2, one square from a new queen. This move decides the whole game.} 4. c7 {Now the pawn goes, and not a move earlier: his king on a6 needs two steps to reach c8, and he only has one left. [%cal Gc7c8]} (4. Kd6 {Escorting the pawn with Kd6 is a move too slow when both runners are this close to queening.} 4... h1=Q 5. c7 5... Qc1 {Lost. His new queen drops to c1 and owns the whole c column, so your pawn can never reach c8.}) (4. Ke7 {Ke7 walks toward the queening square instead of sending the pawn there itself.} 4... h1=Q 5. c7 5... Kb7 {Lost. His king reaches b7 and takes your pawn on c7 next move, while your king on e7 watches from two squares away.}) (4. Kf5 {Chasing his pawn one more time with Kf5 costs you the game exactly when it matters most.} 4... h1=Q 5. Kg5 5... Qxc6 {Lost. His new queen simply eats your pawn on c6, and a bare king has nothing left to fight her with.}) 4... h1=Q {He queens first, on h1, with no check in it. Now it is your turn to answer.} 5. c8=Q+ {A queen of your own arrives on c8, with check. Queen against queen, and neither side can win: the game is drawn. [%csl Gc8]} (5. Kd7 {Running your king to d7 to guard c8, instead of sending the pawn there yourself, is not enough. [%cal Gd7c8]} 5... Qd5+ 6. Ke7 6... Kb7 {Lost. The check pushed your king aside, and now his king on b7 covers c8 while your pawn sits stuck on c7. [%cal Rb7c8][%csl Rc7]}) 1-0


[Event "Pawn endgames: locked pawns"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Role "review"]
[IntendedResult "win"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/J7JDkozw/3sg6k7bw"]
[FEN "8/2K5/4p3/4P3/3k4/8/8/8 w - - 0 1"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/8/2K5/4p3/4P3/3k4/8/8/8_w_-_-_0_1"]

{Locked pawns: whichever king gets beside them first wins the whole ending. One square does it. [%cal Yc7d6]}
1. Kd6 {Kd6 does two jobs at once: it attacks his pawn on e6 and shuts his king out of the centre. [%csl Ge6,Rd4]} (1. Kc6 {Kc6 is one square out, and from there it is your own pawn that falls, not his.} 1... Kxe5 2. Kc5 Ke4 {His king eats the pawn on e5, and now it is his runner that queens. You are lost.}) (1. Kd7 {Kd7 sits too far back to stop him: his king gets to the pawn on e5 well before yours can help.} 1... Kxe5 2. Ke7 Kd5 {The pawn on e5 is gone, and with it the whole game slips away from you.}) (1. Kb6 {Kb6 heads the wrong direction completely, walking away from both pawns instead of toward the fight.} 1... Kxe5 2. Kc5 Ke4 {A pawn down and losing from here, with his own runner already free to queen.}) 1... Kc4 {He runs for the queenside instead of defending. Now collect the pawn he left behind. [%cal Yd6e6]} 2. Kxe6 {The pawn on e6 is yours, and from here it is the same opposition you already know. Now watch it finish. [%csl Ge5][%tail]} (2. Kc6 {Kc6 also wins, but why leave the free pawn on e6 sitting there for later?} 2... Kb4 3. Kd6 Kb5 {You have to come back for that pawn eventually anyway, so Kc6 just wastes a move getting there.}) (2. Ke7 {Ke7 also wins, just by a slower road to the very same pawn on e6, so there is no need to hurry.} 2... Kd5 3. Kf6 Kc5 {You get there in the end, but the pawn on e6 was there for the taking all along.}) (2. Kc7 {Kc7 steps backwards, and the whole game turns around: now his king gets to the pawn first.} 2... Kd5 3. Kb6 Kxe5 {He wins the pawn on e5 with his king, and his passed pawn runs home from there. Lost.}) (2. Kd7 {The same mistake again: Kd7 lets his king come straight back into the centre.} 2... Kd5 3. Kd8 Kxe5 {Lost. His king slips back in and eats the pawn on e5 that you left behind.}) 2... Kc5 3. Kd7 3... Kd5 4. e6 4... Kc4 5. e7 5... Kd4 6. e8=Q {A new queen stands on e8. His king spent the whole game one square behind yours.} 1-0


[Event "Pawn endgames: whose pawn falls first"]
[Result "0-1"]
[Role "exercise"]
[IntendedResult "win"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/J7JDkozw/shBBBw8V"]
[FEN "8/8/8/8/3Kp3/4P3/4k3/8 b - - 0 1"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/8/8/8/8/3Kp3/4P3/4k3/8_b_-_-_0_1"]

{You are Black. The pawns are locked, and one of them is going to fall. Make sure it is his.}
1... Kf3 {Kf3 stands right beside both pawns. Now his pawn on e3 has no guard left at all. [%csl Ge3]} (1... Kd2 {Kd2 lets his king walk straight into the centre instead of defending anything.} 2. Kxe4 {Your pawn on e4 is gone, and the whole game goes with it.}) (1... Kf2 {Kf2 is one square too far to the side to guard anything, and his king simply strolls in.} 2. Kxe4 Ke2 3. Kd4 {A pawn down and lost from here, with his king and pawn far too strong for your bare king.}) (1... Ke1 {Ke1 steps backwards, and it leaves your own pawn on e4 completely undefended.} 2. Kxe4 {Gone: his king simply walks up to e4 and eats it with nothing there to stop him.}) (1... Kd1 {Kd1 is too far from everything that matters here: neither pawn has a guard left near it.} 2. Kxe4 {He simply takes the pawn on e4, with your king stranded two columns away.}) (1... Kf1 {Kf1 is also too far away to defend anything, leaving the pawn on e4 all alone.} 2. Kxe4 {The pawn on e4 falls to his king, and there is nothing left to fight for.}) 2. Kd5 {He goes after your pawn on e4 instead of defending his own. Look again before you rush to guard it.} 2... Kxe3 {Take his pawn on e3! Your own pawn does not need defending, it needs a friend. Now watch them race. [%csl Ge4][%tail]} (2... Ke2 {Stepping back to guard with Ke2 loses the moment: his king simply takes the pawn on e4 instead.} 3. Kxe4 {His king wins the pawn on e4, and his active king and pawn now win the whole game outright. Lost.}) (2... Kf2 {The same idea repeats the same problem: guarding with Kf2 still costs you the pawn on e4.} 3. Kxe4 {His king wins the pawn on e4 the same way, and that active king and pawn win the game. Lost.}) (2... Kg3 {Kg3 goes sideways, and his king simply walks in behind you to take the pawn.} 3. Kxe4 {His king wins the pawn on e4 and stands right in front of it. That is not a hold, it is a win for him. Lost.}) 3. Kd6 3... Kd2 4. Kc5 4... e3 5. Kb4 5... e2 6. Ka3 6... e1=Q {A new queen stands on e1. His own pawn is long gone, and his king never got close to yours.} 0-1


[Event "Pawn endgames: the stalemate trap"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Role "review"]
[IntendedResult "win"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/J7JDkozw/W4daEO0o"]
[FEN "1k6/1P6/8/K7/8/8/8/8 w - - 0 1"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/1k6/1P6/8/K7/8/8/8/8_w_-_-_0_1"]

{A pawn sits on the seventh row with his king in front of it. Careful: one natural-looking move is stalemate. [%cal Ya5a6]}
1. Ka6 {a6, never b6. Now his king has exactly one square left, and he has to give up the corner. [%csl Rb6]} (1. Kb6 {Look at his king on b6: nothing to move, and no check. That is stalemate, and the whole win disappears.}) (1. Kb5 {Kb5 steps away from the pawn entirely, leaving it on b7 for his king to simply take.} 1... Kxb7 {King against bare king now, with the pawn gone. Drawn.}) (1. Kb4 {Kb4 sits too far off, and nothing at all still guards the pawn on b7. [%csl Rb7]} 1... Kxb7 {He simply strolls over and picks the pawn up. Drawn.}) (1. Ka4 {Ka4 wanders off entirely, and the pawn on b7 cannot defend itself.} 1... Kxb7 {Drawn. Nobody is left to guard the pawn on b7, and his king simply walks in and eats it.}) 1... Kc7 {Forced: he has nothing else at all. His king has to give up the last square. [%cal Ya6a7]} 2. Ka7 {Your king takes the queening square. The pawn goes in next move. Now watch it queen. [%csl Gb8][%tail]} (2. b8=Q+ {Queening with check here is a trap: he simply takes the new queen straight back.} 2... Kxb8 {Two bare kings are left on the board, and neither can ever deliver mate. Drawn.}) (2. Ka5 {Ka5 wanders off and lets go of the pawn on b7 completely, with nothing left to guard it.} 2... Kxb7 {It just falls to his king with no defender in sight. Drawn.}) (2. Kb5 {Kb5 lets go of the pawn on b7 too, and from there nothing guards it either. [%csl Rb7]} 2... Kxb7 {Drawn. Your king wandered off, and his simply walked in to eat the pawn.}) 2... Kd8 3. b8=Q+ {A new queen stands on b8, with check. His king was never once allowed near the corner.} 1-0


[Event "Pawn endgames: the corner saves you"]
[Result "0-1"]
[Role "review"]
[IntendedResult "draw"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/J7JDkozw/1PNnyGJa"]
[FEN "8/3k4/8/1K6/P7/8/8/8 w - - 0 1"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/8/3k4/8/1K6/P7/8/8/8_w_-_-_0_1"]

{A pawn down, and his king is in front of it. There is a corner nearby that saves the game for you.}
1. Kb6 {He swings his king round the side, onto b6. Choose your square very carefully now.} 1... Kc8 {Kc8 is the only square. You are heading for the corner right in front of his pawn. [%csl Ga8]} (1... Kd6 {Kd6 goes for the pawn directly, but you are one move short of catching it.} 2. a5 Kd5 3. a6 {His pawn queens on a8 unopposed, and you are lost.}) (1... Kd8 {Kd8 is the wrong corner: that one is much too far away to help.} 2. Kb7 Kd7 3. a5 {His king guards the road, and the pawn simply walks in.}) (1... Ke7 {Ke7 wanders away from the action, and the pawn simply runs past.} 2. a5 Kd6 3. a6 {You cannot catch it now: the pawn runs on while your king is stuck on the wrong side.}) 2. a5 {He pushes to a5. Stay on the plan you already chose.} 2... Kb8 {Kb8 stands straight in front of the pawn, right on the road to the corner.} (2... Kd7 {Kd7 steps you right back out of the corner you had just reached.} 3. a6 Kd6 4. a7 {The pawn reaches a8 and queens, with your king a whole move too slow to stop it.}) (2... Kd8 {Kd8 again: the corner you want is b8, not this one.} 3. a6 Kd7 4. a7 {Too far away now to matter: the pawn simply strolls past your stranded king.}) 3. a6 {One more push, to a6. Last step before the corner decides it.} 3... Ka8 {The corner at last. His king can never push you off a8, so now watch him try to make progress anyway. [%tail][%csl Ga8][%csl Gb8]} (3... Kc8 {Kc8 is one square out of the corner, and the pawn goes straight past you to queen.} 4. a7 Kd7 5. a8=Q {A new queen appears on a8, and the game is over before your king ever arrives.}) 4. Kc7 4... Ka7 {He had to abandon b6 to try anything at all, and now your king slips to a7, right beside his pawn on a6.} 5. Kc6 5... Kxa6 {His last pawn falls on a6. Two bare kings can never mate each other, so the game is a draw. [%csl Ga6]} 0-1


[Event "Pawn endgames: the breakthrough"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Role "teaching"]
[IntendedResult "win"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/J7JDkozw/eeC8ZYaf"]
[FEN "7k/ppp5/8/PPP5/8/8/8/7K w - - 0 1"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/7k/ppp5/8/PPP5/8/8/8/7K_w_-_-_0_1"]

{Three pawns each, and nothing has passed yet. Learn the breakthrough: the middle pawn goes first, and a road opens behind it. [%cal Yb5b6]}
1. b6 {b6 is the spearhead: whichever of his pawns takes it, a road opens up for one of your others. [%csl Ga5,Gc5]} (1. Kg2 {Kg2 also wins, since the breakthrough on b6 does not run away, but there is no reason at all to wait for it.} 1... Kg7 2. b6 axb6 3. c6 {He plays the same defence two moves later, and the breakthrough on c6 still cracks it open all the same.}) (1. Kh2 {Kh2 is a quiet move, and it gives him the one move he needed to lock everything down.} 1... b6 {Now every pawn is locked in place, and nothing can ever break through. Drawn.}) (1. Kg1 {The same waiting move as Kh2, and it draws the same reply from him.} 1... b6 {He blocks with his own b6, and the breakthrough you needed is gone for good.}) (1. c6 {c6 is the wrong pawn to push: it has no friend behind it to open a road.} 1... bxc6 2. bxc6 Kg8 {Your pawns are stuck, and his king marches straight in to eat them. You are losing.}) (1. a6 {a6 is an outside pawn on the wing, and it cannot break through the row on its own.} 1... bxa6 2. bxa6 Kg7 {The same story: your pawns freeze solid on the fifth row, and the game slips away from you.}) 1... cxb6 {He recaptures with the c pawn. Now push the far pawn instead. [%cal Ya5a6]} 2. a6 {Look at c5 now: once he takes again, nothing at all stands in its way. [%csl Gc5]} (2. axb6 {Recapturing with axb6 is exactly what he wanted: it trades pawns instead of breaking the road open.} 2... axb6 3. cxb6 Kg7 {Nothing is passed any more once the trade goes through, and the position is dead level. Drawn.}) (2. cxb6 {The other recapture has the same problem: it trades instead of breaking through.} 2... axb6 3. axb6 Kg7 {All six pawns come off the board in the trades, and the position is completely level.}) (2. c6 {c6 is the wrong runner here: he takes it, and your last pawn is left stuck behind.} 2... bxc6 3. axb6 axb6 {Your last pawn is stuck behind his, and his two remaining pawns run all the way home. Lost.}) (2. Kg2 {A quiet king move here loses on the spot, right when the breakthrough needed finishing.} 2... bxa5 {His pawn is off and running down the open file, and your king can never catch it. Lost.}) 2... bxa6 {He had to recapture again. Now run with the c pawn. [%cal Yc5c6]} 3. c6 {And the pawn walks straight in: his king is five whole squares too far away to stop it. Now watch it queen. [%cal Gc6c8][%tail]} (3. cxb6 {cxb6 grabs a pawn but gives up the runner that was about to walk home.} 3... axb6 4. Kg2 a5 {His two pawns run home together, and yours is gone for nothing. Lost.}) (3. Kg2 {The pawn on c6 had to run right now, not wait behind a quiet king move that gives him time.} 3... bxc5 {His pawns stay healthy and connected, yours is gone, and you are the one losing now.}) 3... Kg7 4. c7 4... Kf6 5. c8=Q {A new queen stands on c8. The breakthrough opened the road, and his king never even reached it.} 1-0


[Event "Pawn endgames: find the breakthrough"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Role "exercise"]
[IntendedResult "win"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/J7JDkozw/LQJp8rLq"]
[FEN "8/8/1p6/pP5p/P1P1p1k1/8/5K2/8 b - - 0 42"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/8/8/1p6/pP5p/P1P1p1k1/8/5K2/8_b_-_-_0_42"]

{From a real game: his king is marching over toward your pawns. One pawn move settles the whole thing.}
42... Kf5 {He steps closer, to f5, closing in on your queenside pawns. Take your time here: only one move wins.} 43. c5 {The breakthrough! It looks like a free pawn for him, but watch what happens on b5 afterwards. [%csl Gb5]} (43. Ke3 {One quiet king move here and the whole breakthrough collapses before it even starts.} 43... Ke5 44. Kf2 Kd4 45. Kg3 Kxc4 {His king walks straight into your queenside and eats everything in sight. You are lost.}) (43. Kg3 {Kg3 chases his h pawn while his own king eats yours on the other wing.} 43... Ke5 44. Kh4 Kd6 {He gets clean through on the queenside while your king was busy elsewhere. You are lost.}) (43. Kg2 {Kg2 steps backwards, and the one moment for the breakthrough on c5 has already gone.} 43... Ke6 44. Kh3 Kd6 {His king arrives at your queenside pawns first, and there is nothing left to defend them. Lost.}) (43. Ke2 {Waiting with Ke2 loses here: the breakthrough on c5 had to happen on this very move.} 43... Ke6 44. Kf2 Kd6 {He walks up and takes the c pawn, and the whole game falls with it.}) 43... bxc5 {He took with the b pawn, and that was the mistake. Now the road for your last pawn is wide open.} 44. b6 {Nothing can stop it now: his king is three whole columns too far away. Now watch the pawn walk home. [%cal Gb6b8][%tail]} (44. Ke3 {Waiting one move with Ke3 lets his king catch the pawn after all.} 44... Ke5 45. b6 Kd6 46. b7 Kc7 {His king gets to the pawn in time to stop it queening. Lost.}) (44. Kg3 {The same wasted king move again, and it costs the game the same way.} 44... Ke5 45. b6 Kd6 {Too late now: his king reaches the pawn before yours can ever get there.}) 44... Ke5 45. b7 45... Kd4 46. b8=Q {A new queen stands on b8. His king never got within a country mile of it.} 1-0


[Event "Pawn endgames: count the race"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Role "exercise"]
[IntendedResult "win"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/J7JDkozw/D83WpSkT"]
[FEN "8/5p2/1p1k2p1/6Pp/p1PK1P1P/P7/8/8 b - - 2 40"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/8/5p2/1p1k2p1/6Pp/p1PK1P1P/P7/8/8_b_-_-_2_40"]

{Four real games now, and no hints this time. First: your c pawn is attacked. Count the whole race before you decide how to defend it.}
40... Kc6 {He steps up to win the c pawn. Is defending it really the most important thing right now?} 41. Ke5 {Let him take the pawn. Your king is racing for his kingside pawns instead, and that race you win. [%csl Rc4]} (41. Kd3 {Defending the pawn with Kd3 saves it, but it also saves his position: the race you needed to win never starts.} 41... Kd6 42. Kd4 Ke6 {Nobody can make progress from here: both kings are frozen guarding their own pawn. Drawn.}) (41. Ke4 {Ke4 keeps your king in the middle, but the race for his pawns needed to start this very move.} 41... Kc5 42. Kd3 Kc6 {He shuffles his king back to d3 and holds the position without ever needing to move forward. Drawn.}) (41. Ke3 {Ke3 steps backwards, and the race you needed to start is over before it ever began.} 41... Kc5 42. Kd3 Kc6 {Nothing left to do here: neither king dares step forward. Drawn.}) (41. Kc3 {The same waiting idea, just on the other wing, and it costs you the same way.} 41... Kc5 42. Kd3 Kc6 {A draw: neither king can step forward without losing its own pawn race.}) (41. f5 {Breaking on the kingside first with f5 lets his king walk straight over to your side instead.} 41... Kd6 42. f6 Kc6 {He holds everything together on the kingside, and the win you had slips away.}) (41. c5 {Pushing the c pawn he was about to take anyway just hands him the same pawn for free.} 41... b5 42. Kd3 Kxc5 {He ends up a pawn up on the queenside, and you are the one losing the race now.}) 41... Kc5 {He goes straight for the pawn on c4. Keep your own king running for the kingside.} 42. Kf6 {Your king is already on the wrong side of the board for him to stop it now.} (42. f5 {Trading with f5 hands everything back that the race had already won for you.} 42... Kxc4 43. fxg6 fxg6 {The pawns come off in the trade, and what is left is only a draw.}) (42. Ke4 {Turning back to defend with Ke4 is far too slow now that the race is on.} 42... Kxc4 43. Kf3 b5 {His pawn runs all the way home, and yours never even gets close to queening. Lost.}) 42... Kxc4 {He has his own pawn back now. You are about to take two of his in return.} 43. Kxf7 {You gave one pawn and took two, and your king on f7 now stands right beside his pawn on g6. Now watch it fall too. [%csl Gg6][%tail]} (43. f5 {Trading with f5 at this exact moment gives back everything the count had already won.} 43... gxf5 44. Kxf7 f4 {Both sides queen at the same moment. Dead level, and the whole race was run for nothing.}) (43. Kg7 {Going round the other way with Kg7 costs the one tempo you actually needed.} 43... b5 44. Kxf7 b4 {Both sides queen now, at the same time, and the win you were counting on has slipped away.}) (43. Ke7 {The same wasted move, one square along, and it costs you exactly the same way.} 43... b5 44. Kxf7 b4 {One move too slow to win the race outright, so it settles for only a draw.}) (43. Ke5 {Stepping back towards his king with Ke5 turns the whole race against you.} 43... b5 44. Kf6 b4 {His pawn reaches the last row and queens, and you are lost.}) 43... Kb3 44. Kxg6 44... Kxa3 45. f5 45... Kb3 46. Kxh5 46... a3 47. g6 47... a2 48. g7 48... a1=Q 49. g8=Q+ {Your own queen arrives on g8 with check, a tempo and a pawn to the good. That is how you count a race.} 1-0


[Event "Pawn endgames: trade into the win"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Role "exercise"]
[IntendedResult "win"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/J7JDkozw/RigoYrBd"]
[FEN "8/8/8/6p1/7p/5KPP/8/5k2 w - - 1 53"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/8/8/8/6p1/7p/5KPP/8/5k2_w_-_-_1_53"]

{Two pawns each, and his king is a long way from either of them. One capture wins the whole ending.}
53. gxh4 {Take on h4! It leaves one pawn each, and yours is the one with a king standing right beside it.} (53. Kg4 {Walking round the pawns with Kg4 misses the point: the trade on h4 was what won the game.} 53... hxg3 54. Kxg3 Kg1 {One pawn each, and neither side can ever get theirs to queen. Drawn.}) (53. g4 {Pushing past with g4 locks your own pawns together instead of trading them off.} 53... Kg1 54. Ke2 Kg2 {His h pawn is now the only runner left on the board, and you are lost.}) (53. Ke4 {The king wanders off with Ke4, and the pawns are left to sort themselves out alone.} 53... hxg3 54. Kf5 g2 {That pawn reaches g1 and queens, with your king still a column away. You are lost.}) (53. Ke3 {The same problem again: the trade on h4 was the whole idea, and Ke3 skips right past it.} 53... hxg3 54. Kd2 g2 {One more step and his pawn queens, with your king nowhere close enough to stop it.}) 53... gxh4 {He takes back on h4. Now go and get that pawn with your king.} 54. Kg4 {Straight at the pawn. You take it next move, and your own h pawn walks home behind you. Now watch it queen. [%cal Gg4h4,Gh3h8][%tail]} (54. Kf4 {Kf4 is one square out, and his king arrives at the pawn just in time to help.} 54... Ke2 55. Ke4 Kd1 {He gets his king back in time to help defend, and the ending settles for only a draw.}) (54. Ke3 {Ke3 steps backwards, and the h pawn is left completely safe from your king.} 54... Ke1 55. Kd3 Kd1 {Drawn. His king slips back in front of the pawn, and yours can never push him out.}) (54. Ke4 {Ke4 goes away from the pawn entirely, right when your king needed to be heading for it.} 54... Ke1 55. Kd3 Kd1 {His king holds the pawn safely on h4, and the position is a draw.}) 54... Ke2 55. Kxh4 55... Kf3 56. Kg5 56... Kg3 57. h4 57... Kf3 58. h5 58... Ke4 59. h6 59... Kd5 60. h7 60... Kc6 61. h8=Q {A new queen stands on h8. His king never got anywhere near either pawn.} 1-0


[Event "Pawn endgames: the rook pawn needs the corner"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Role "review"]
[IntendedResult "win"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/J7JDkozw/1LhCNokF"]
[FEN "8/3k4/5K2/8/7P/8/8/8 b - - 0 56"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/8/3k4/5K2/8/7P/8/8/8_b_-_-_0_56"]

{Last one. A rook pawn is the fussy kind: your king must own the corner before the pawn ever runs.}
56... Ke8 {He runs for the corner on h8. If he ever gets there, this whole ending is a draw.} 57. Kg7 {Kg7 takes the corner square first. It is yours now, and his king can never come anywhere near it. [%csl Gh8]} (57. h5 {Running the pawn alone with h5 lets him stroll straight into the corner instead.} 57... Kf8 58. h6 Kg8 {He sits down on h8, and you can never push him back out. Drawn.}) (57. Kg6 {Kg6 is one square short, and he still slips into the corner ahead of you.} 57... Kf8 58. h5 Kg8 {The corner is his now, and there is no way left to push him back out. Drawn.}) (57. Kf5 {Kf5 steps backwards, and the road to h8 is wide open for him again.} 57... Ke7 58. h5 Kf7 {He gets in front of the pawn on f6, and it can never pass him. Drawn.}) (57. Ke6 {Ke6 chases his king instead of taking the corner that actually matters.} 57... Kf8 58. h5 Kg7 {He blocks the pawn on g7 there forever, and it never gets another chance to run.}) 57... Ke7 {He tries to come back for the corner. Too late for that now.} 58. h5 {Now the pawn runs, with your king already sitting on the queening corner. Now watch it walk home. [%cal Gh5h8][%tail]} (58. Kg6 {Letting go of the corner with Kg6 lets him run straight back to it.} 58... Kf8 59. h5 Kg8 {Drawn. He is back in the corner, and your king can never pry him out.}) (58. Kh6 {Kh6 goes sideways, and that gives his king the time it needed to come back.} 58... Kf6 59. h5 Kf7 {He walks straight back into the corner, and your king can never dislodge him. Drawn.}) (58. Kh7 {Kh7 is the wrong corner square, and it lets his king slip back in behind you.} 58... Kf6 59. h5 Kf7 {A draw: his king strolls back in front of the pawn and holds it there forever.}) (58. Kg8 {Kg8 steps backwards, when your king was already sitting perfectly.} 58... Kf6 59. h5 Kg5 {He picks the pawn straight up off g5, and the position is a draw.}) 58... Kd6 59. h6 59... Ke5 60. h7 60... Kf5 61. h8=Q {A new queen stands on h8. The corner was yours long before the pawn even ran.} 1-0


[Event "Pawn endgames: the king walks in"]
[Result "0-1"]
[Role "exercise"]
[IntendedResult "win"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/J7JDkozw/35hddu04"]
[FEN "8/2p3p1/5k1p/p1p5/P2pP3/1P6/2P2KPP/8 w - - 5 44"]
[SetUp "1"]
