{One more real game, and you are Black this time. Every pawn move looks useful here. Only one of them actually wins.}
44. h4 {He grabs a little space on the kingside, pushing to h4. Now find the move that decides everything.} 44... Ke5 {Your king marches straight into the centre. His pawn on e4 cannot be defended twice over. [%csl Ge4]} (44... c4 {Breaking with c4 on the queenside only hands him a pawn he simply takes.} 45. bxc4 h5 46. Kg3 Ke5 {Level: the king was the piece that had to move here, not a pawn, and the position stays balanced.}) (44... g5 {A pawn break with g5 wastes the one moment your king needed to move instead.} 45. hxg5+ Kxg5 46. Kf3 {His king reaches e4 first and yours never gets there at all. You are losing.}) (44... d3 {Pushing your best pawn to d3 shoves it straight into his hands.} 45. cxd3 Ke5 46. Ke3 {The passed pawn on d4 is gone, and he holds everything else together too. Lost.}) (44... h5 {h5 looks useful, but the king had to move to e5 right now, not a pawn.} 45. Ke2 c4 46. b4 {He gets his own passed pawn running, and you are the one losing.}) 45. Ke2 {He hurries his king all the way across the board to hold the pawn on e4.} 45... Kxe4 {Too late for him. His pawn on e4 is gone, and you are a clean pawn up with a runner on d4 his king can never get near alone. [%csl Gd4]} (45... c4 {Playing c4 now only helps him: it opens the position for his own pieces instead of yours.} 46. bxc4 Kxe4 47. h5 {His h pawn runs unopposed down the column, and the win you had has gone.}) (45... g5 {It is too late for pawn moves here: the king had to take on e4 immediately.} 46. hxg5 hxg5 47. Kd3 {His king reaches c4 and eats his way through your queenside. Lost.}) (45... Kf4 {Walking past the pawn with Kf4 instead of simply taking it lets him hold everything.} 46. Kd3 g6 47. Kc4 {He gets his king into your queenside pawns, and now you are the one losing.}) 0-1
